  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale:\nThis approach combines randomized sampling with constrained optimization to explore the function space effectively. Unlike the previous deterministic methods, it uses probabilistic perturbations to surmount known local optima and explore regions with lower C5 values. The core idea is to represent the step function as a ternary state vector (pattern of 0, 0.5, or 1 values) maintaining the area constraint. This simplifies computation while preserving many solution candidates. We then optimize over projected ternary configurations using a trust-region method with dynamic scaling of perturbations. The method addresses known local optimums by adaptive randomization and exploits 2D Fourier structure to inform blocking in the grid.\n\n2. Mathematical specification:\nThe optimization variable h is represented as a flattened vector within [0,1] with sum 1. The objective is the normalized maximum circular correlation (computed as `np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode='full').max() * dx`). The optimization uses modified projected gradients with:\n\n- Iterative clipping projection: s.t. \u2211h[i] = 1 and 0\u2264h[i]\u22641\n- Lagrange multipliers for the equality constraint\n- Dynamic regularization based on gradient curvature\n\nThe key innovation is the randomization pattern defined by sine waves and Fourier interactions at x=1, designed to escape the ring-shaped overlap pattern observed in the reference solution.\n\n3. Implementation procedure:\n1. Input: parent_h_values (seed if available)\n2. Precompute local grid: grid = [0,1,...,n] with n=512. Scale factor \u03c3=0.05\n3. Projection function def project(h):\n   - Clip to [0,1]\n   - Scale to sum(n/2)\n4. Randomization pattern:\n   def perturb(h, grid, level):\n     - This function scales based on gradient magnitude and introduces sine/discontinuity patterns to perturb\n   return clipped h\n\n5. Optimization loop:\n   initialize best_f = inf\n   for _ in range(5000): (within budget)\n     Generate candidate = perturb(parent, grid, alpha) * scale_factor\n     Run trust-region optimization using:\n        obj = f(c) = max_lag(np.correlate(c,1-c,...)\n        cons = \u2211c = n/2, 0\u2264c\u22641\n     Trust region options: max_iter=50, ftol=1e-8\n     Update best candidate\n\n6. Special cases:\n   - Use continuation from results of initial grid searches\n   - Terminal conditions on time/budget\n   - Gradient computation via finite-differences\n\n4. Search choices:\n   - Perturbation strategy: adaptive sine-based randomized distribution\n   - Regularization: initially L2 weight 0.1, decay to 0.001 over 5000 iterations\n   - Candidate scaling: alpha in range [0.9,0.01] guided by function progress\n   - Specialized linear warp for discontinuity at x=1\n\n5. Compute budget:\n   - Total operations ~2.8e6 function evaluations\n   - Trust-region + gradient evaluation per candidate\n   - Uses mixed-precision arithmetic\n   - Time allocation: 60% final optimization, 40% exploration\n\n6. Validation contract:\n   - Input validation: h[i] in [0,1] with sum(n/2)\n   - Objective evaluation: np.correlate(...).max() * dx\n   - Fall-back: arbitrary parent configuration\n   - Timeline: 12 iterations/seconds \n   - Termination with best_c and h_vectors\n\nThe method constructs valid candidates by:\n1. Initializing from a ternary parent\n2. Applying inverse-linear sine modulation (ds/dt = d*sin(k+t) ) \n3. Performing projected gradient steps with trust-region\n4. Maintaining history for best solution\n5. Adaptive chaos parameterization of None",
